[[File:Official Sign Compilation.png|thumb]]
Signs are a component of seals, special shapes drawn with [[Conjuring Ink|conjuring ink]] through which [[Magic|magic]] can be harnessed in the form of [[Spells|spells]]. Signs control what form a spell will take. They serve as modifiers, allowing the effect of a spell to be altered. Each sign has a unique effect with it can contribute to a spell.

__TOC__

==Sign Categories==
Based on their design and behavior, signs can be broken down into four groups; directional signs, semi-directional signs, non-directional signs, and asymmetric signs. These groups are never mentioned, named, or explained within the source material. However, breaking signs into these categories is incredibly useful for understanding how different signs function, so they will be used within this article.

===Directional Signs===
[[File:Sign Length Demo.png|200px|thumb|right|Changing sign length to alter spell direction]]
Directional signs can manifest their effect in a specific direction. Some examples of directional signs include [[Signs Explained#Columns|'''Columns''']] and [[Signs Explained#Pulling|'''Pulling''']]. All directional signs possess bilateral symmetry but lack radial symmetry. By changing the angle or length of directional signs, one can influence in which direction a spell will manifest. Spells which make use of this property include [[Skysoaring Seal]] and the [[Rising Platform of Water]].

===Semi-Directional Signs===
Semi-directional signs can be inverted yet lack a distinct direction for their effect. Some examples of semi-directional signs include [[Signs Explained#Crushing|'''Crushing''']] and [[Signs Explained#Empowerment|'''Empowerment''']]. Most semi-directional signs possess bilateral symmetry but lack radial symmetry, similar to directional signs. However, some do possess radial symmetry, like [[Signs Explained#Rain|'''Rain''']] or [[Signs Explained#Enlarge|'''Enlarge''']]. Changing their size will only alter the strength of their effect, not direction. The direction of the sign doesn't alter the direction of the spell, but if the sign is inverted (facing outwards), the effect of the sign will be the opposite of what it is usually. For semi-directional signs that have radial symmetry, the sign must be flipped inside-out in order for it to be inverted. A pair of spells that makes use of the inversion of semi-directional signs are [[Wall Breaker Seal|Wall Breaker]] and [[Integration]], with Wall Breaker using '''Crushing''' to pulverize while Integration inverts them to reform.

===Non-Directional Signs===
Non-directional signs lack a direction for their effect and act the same regardless of the direction they point. Examples of non-directional signs include [[Signs Explained#Stability and Level Planes|'''Stability and Level Planes''']] and [[Signs Explained#Crosshair|'''Crosshair''']]. Lacking bilateral symmetry but possessing radial symmetry, non-directional signs lack a distinct front, back, left, or right. As a result, it isn't possible to invert these signs, as there is no way to have them point inward, as they lack front with which to point.

===Asymmetric Signs===
Lacking symmetry of any kind, asymmetric signs are unpredictable and largely unknown. It is not known if inverting, mirroring, or angling them changes their behavior.

